# Azure Deployment Plan

## Goal

Deploy the London Underground Timetable application to Azure App Service as a Linux Node.js web app.

## Project Information

- Technology stack: Node.js, Express, Socket.io, static assets in `public/`
- Application type: Server-rendered web app with live websocket updates
- Runtime dependencies: `express`, `socket.io`
- External dependencies: TfL public APIs over HTTPS
- Hosting recommendation: Azure App Service for Linux Web App

## Azure Resources Architecture

```mermaid
graph TD
    User[Browser Clients] --> WebApp[Azure App Service Linux Web App]
    WebApp --> TfL[TfL Public API]
    WebApp --> Static[public/* assets]
```

## Why App Service

- The app is already an HTTP server and listens on `PORT`.
- Socket.io works on App Service when WebSockets are enabled.
- No container build is required.

## Provisioning Files

- `infra/main.bicep`: App Service plan plus Linux Web App
- `infra/main.parameters.json`: Default deployment parameters

## Deployment Steps

1. Create or choose a resource group.
2. Run `az deployment group what-if` against `infra/main.bicep`, passing `prefix=<app-name-prefix>`.
3. Run `az deployment group create` to provision the App Service plan and web app as `<app-name-prefix>-web`.
4. Deploy the app package with `az webapp deploy --type zip` using `london-underground-timetable-1.0.0.zip` and the generated web app name.
5. Browse the deployed hostname from the Bicep outputs.

## Required App Service Settings

- WebSockets enabled
- Always On enabled
- Node runtime `~20`
- Build during deployment enabled

## Notes

- A stray VS Code workspace file was removed from `public/` so it is not exposed in production.
- The current repo already contains verified `.tgz` and `.zip` release artifacts.
